94aB52a9a77c\"><img src=\"https://link.axios.com/img/6a14a774a4e0095e0a03c94asjx2s.96lc/754fb478.gif\" alt=\"\" border=\"0\" /></a></td>\r\n<td style=\"padding-right:3.5%\" align=\"right\"> <a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/47958580.428448/aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cuYXhpb3MuY29tL25ld3NsZXR0ZXJzL2F4aW9zLWZpbmlzaC1saW5lLTRjZTI5NzU4LWEyNDMtNDAyMy1hODE4LTU2MmY2N2ZlNDFlMC5odG1sP3V0bV9zb3VyY2U9bmV3c2xldHRlciZ1dG1fbWVkaXVtPWVtYWlsJnV0bV9jYW1wYWlnbj1uZXdzbGV0dGVyX2F4aW9zZmluaXNobGluZSZzdHJlYW09dG9w/6a14a774a4e0095e0a03c94aB7a5ae335\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"b\" style=\"color:#222;font-size:13px;font-family:'gordita',Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;text-decoration:underline\">View in browser</a> </td>\r\n</tr>\r\n</table>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr>\r\n<td height=\"30\" style=\"height:30px;font-size:0\">&nbsp;</td>\r\n</tr>  <tr>\r\n<td style=\"text-align:center;line-height:1.3\"> </td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr>\r\n<td height=\"16\" style=\"height:16px;font-size:0\">&nbsp;</td>\r\n</tr><tr>\r\n<td style=\"text-align:center;line-height:1\">\r\n<span class=\"s\" style=\"font-size:44px;font-family:'gorditamedium',Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;color:#008dc8;letter-spacing:-1px;line-height:1\"> Axios Finish Line </span>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr>\r\n<td style=\"text-align:center;line-height:1.5;padding-bottom:35px;padding-top:10px\">\r\n<span class=\"b\" style=\"font-size:13px;color:#222;font-family:'gorditamedium',Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif\">By  Mike Allen and Erica Pandey and Jim VandeHei  <span style=\"margin:0 6px\">&middot;</span> Oct 15, 2026 </span>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr><tr>\r\n<td class=\"v\" style=\"font-size:17px;font-family:Georgia,serif;line-height:1.75;color:#222;letter-spacing:-0.1px;padding:0 3.5%\"><p><strong>Welcome back! </strong><em>Smart Brevity™ count: 318 words … 1 min. Copy edited by Amy Stern.</em></p></td>\r\n</tr><tr>\r\n<td height=\"10\" style=\"height:10px;font-size:0;border-bottom:10px solid #f5f5f5\">&nbsp;</td>\r\n</tr>      <tr>\r\n<td height=\"10\" style=\"height:10px;font-size:0\">&nbsp;</td>\r\n</tr><tr>\r\n<td style=\"padding:20px 3.5% 10px;line-height:1.5\">\r\n<span class=\"b k\" style=\"font-size:27px;font-family:'gorditamedium',Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;color:#222;letter-spacing:-0.1px;line-height:1.5\">  1 big thing: Upside of logging on </span>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>   <tr>\r\n<td height=\"10\" style=\"height:10px;font-size:0\">&nbsp;</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr>\r\n<td> <img src=\"https://images.axios.com/pZ5TCtn7VJnRBX457FudROendT8=/1920x1080/smart/2024/09/19/183134-1726770694569.jpg\" width=\"600\" style=\"width:100%\" id=\"item02\" alt=\"Illustration of two phones displaying two forming a heart shape on the screens.\"> </td>\r\n</tr><tr>\r\n<td height=\"10\" style=\"height:10px;font-size:0\">\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr>\r\n<td style=\"padding:0 3.5%;line-height:1.3\">\r\n<span class=\"b\" style=\"font-size:13px;color:#848484\"> <p>Illustration: Allie Carl/Axios</p> </span>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr><tr>\r\n<td height=\"15\" style=\"height:15px;font-size:0\">&nbsp;</td>\r\n</tr> <tr>\r\n<td class=\"v\" style=\"padding:0 3.5%;line-height:1.75;font-size:17px;font-family:Georgia,serif;color:#222;letter-spacing:-0.1px\"><p><strong>Technology often gets blamed</strong> for making us lonelier and more isolated. But the internet can also help us feel<em> less</em> alone by bringing people together.</p><ul><li><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Online communities connect people who might never otherwise cross paths — or even speak the same language — through shared interests and experiences.</li></ul><p>🧮 <strong>By the numbers:</strong> Some 4 in 10 Americans — across all age groups — belong to an online community, according to a new <a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/47958580.428448/aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cucGV3cmVzZWFyY2gub3JnL2ludGVybmV0LzIwMjYvMTAvMTQvb25saW5lLWNvbW11bml0eS1hbmQtY29ubmVjdGlvbi1pbi1hbWVyaWNhLz91dG1fc291cmNlPW5ld3NsZXR0ZXImdXRtX21lZGl1bT1lbWFpbCZ1dG1fY2FtcGFpZ249bmV3c2xldHRlcl9heGlvc2ZpbmlzaGxpbmUmc3RyZWFtPXRvcA/6a14a774a4e0095e0a03c94aBce4200d0\" target=\"_blank\">Pew Research Center survey</a>. Think online book clubs, parenting forums, TV show discussion groups or cancer support groups.</p><ul><li><strong>70% of members</strong> say these communities have added value to their lives, and 63% say their most-visited group offers something they couldn't find offline.</li><li><strong>36% say their</strong> online community also brings people together through in-person gatherings.</li></ul><p><strong>Zoom in:</strong> Research reveals some of the benefits of these groups.</p><ul><li><strong>A 2025 </strong><a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/47958580.428448/aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cubmF0dXJlLmNvbS9hcnRpY2xlcy9zNDQyNzEtMDI1LTAwMjE3LTY_dXRtX3NvdXJjZT1uZXdzbGV0dGVyJnV0bV9tZWRpdW09ZW1haWwmdXRtX2NhbXBhaWduPW5ld3NsZXR0ZXJfYXhpb3NmaW5pc2hsaW5lJnN0cmVhbT10b3A/6a14a774a4e0095e0a03c94aBcbe4926c\" target=\"_blank\"><strong>review</strong></a> of 100 studies found that online support groups can help people with chronic illnesses better adjust to their conditions.</li></ul><p><strong>🔎 The intrigue:</strong> Online socializing is often dismissed as a less-than-ideal substitute for in-person interaction. But online and in-person relationships may actually complement each other.</p><ul><li><strong>A small 2026 </strong><a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/47958580.428448/aHR0cHM6Ly9mb3JtYXRpdmUuam1pci5vcmcvMjAyNi8xL2U5MDc1MS8_dXRtX3NvdXJjZT1uZXdzbGV0dGVyJnV0bV9tZWRpdW09ZW1haWwmdXRtX2NhbXBhaWduPW5ld3NsZXR0ZXJfYXhpb3NmaW5pc2hsaW5lJnN0cmVhbT10b3A/6a14a774a4e0095e0a03c94aB967a9ed5\" target=\"_blank\"><strong>study</strong></a> found that in-person support matters more for happiness. But people with strong online support also tend to have stronger real-life connections.</li></ul><p><strong>The bottom line: </strong>Find your corner of the internet.</p></td>\r\n</tr> <tr><td align=\"center\" style=\"padding:20px 3.5% 25px\">   <a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/47958580.428448/aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cuZmFjZWJvb2suY29tL3NoYXJlci5waHA_